<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Osman I</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1324</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Osman I was the founder of the Ottoman dynasty, a Turkoman chieftain of the Kayı clan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, active around 1300. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> lay on the frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and attracted ghazi warriors. His victory over a Byzantine force at Bapheus in 1301 established the military credibility on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> was built.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osman-i.jpg" alt="Osman I" width="263" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ottoman miniature painting · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Osman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Very little is certain about Osman. No written source survives from his reign, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> did not record his life until the fifteenth century, more than a hundred years after his death. One historian has called the period of his life a black hole. The oldest accounts include the Destan-ı Tevarih-i Al-i Osman of Ahmedî, the Behcetü't-Tevârîh of Şükrullah and the Tevarih-i Âl-i Osman of Âşıkpaşazâde, and they survive in copies. Later Ottoman tradition made his ancestors descendants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kay-tribe">Kayı tribe</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, but many scholars regard that as a later fabrication meant to strengthen dynastic legitimacy. His name may originally have been Turkish, perhaps Atman, and later changed to the Arabic ʿOsmān.</p><p>His date of birth is unknown. Some sources give 13 February 1258, and the sixteenth-century historian Kemalpaşazade gave 1254/5. The few sources agree that he was born at Söğüt, which his father Ertuğrul took as the capital of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a>. According to Ottoman tradition, Ertuğrul pledged allegiance to the Anatolian Seljuks, who gave him Söğüt on the Byzantine frontier, though that connection was largely invented by court chroniclers a century later. The Karamanid historian Shikari called Osman a lowborn Yörük and shepherd.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Ottoman principality was one of many Anatolian beyliks that emerged in the second half of the thirteenth century. It lay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a>, well placed for attacks on the vulnerable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, and Söğüt stood on a defensible hill on the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a>. Osman became emir upon his father's death, around 1281. Some historians think the succession was not peaceful, and that he killed his uncle Dündar Bey for disobedience. Almost nothing is known of his early activities except that he controlled the region around Söğüt and raided Byzantine lands from there.</p><p>The first datable event is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bapheus">Battle of Bapheus</a>, in which he defeated a Byzantine force sent against him. He appears to have enlarged his territory at Byzantine expense while avoiding conflict with his more powerful Turkish neighbours. His conquests included Bilecik, Yenişehir, İnegöl and Yarhisar, and Yenişehir became the capital. After a victory near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a> he began to settle his forces closer to Byzantine areas. By 1308 he had extended his control north along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sakarya-river">Sakarya River</a> and south-west towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a>.</p><p>He married the daughter of the dervish leader Sheikh Edebali, and later Ottoman writers told a story of a dream he had in the sheikh's house. Osman died in 1323 or 1324. His small Turkoman principality became a vast empire in the centuries after his death, which lasted until the sultanate was abolished in 1922.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Osman I” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>One of these, the beylik of <b>Osman I</b>, a Turkoman chieftain traditionally said to belong to the Kayı tribe of the Oghuz Turks, lay in the Bithynian borderlands near Bursa, directly on the frontier between the Islamic and Byzantine worlds.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Söğüt, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Söğüt, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.02,30.18&amp;year=1314" aria-label="Söğüt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5293 732 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Osman I"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5743 1002h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5743 1002h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.02,30.18&amp;year=1314">What was here in 1314? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
